      "text": "To find the probability that both rolls are 6 given that at least one roll is 6, we use the definition of conditional probability:\n\n$$ P(A | B) = \\frac{P(A \\cap B)}{P(B)} $$\n\nLet $A$ be the event that both rolls are 6.\nLet $B$ be the event that at least one roll is 6.\n\n**Step 1: Determine the total number of outcomes.**\nA fair six-sided die rolled twice has $6 \\times 6 = 36$ possible outcomes.\n\n**Step 2: Determine the number of outcomes for event $B$ (at least one 6).**\nThe outcomes where at least one roll is 6 are:\n- First roll is 6: $(6,1), (6,2), (6,3), (6,4), (6,5), (6,6)$ \u2014 6 outcomes\n- Second roll is 6: $(1,6), (2,6), (3,6), (4,6), (5,6), (6,6)$ \u2014 6 outcomes\n\nNote that the outcome $(6,6)$ is counted in both lists. To avoid double-counting, we sum the unique outcomes:\n$$ 6 + 6 - 1 = 11 $$\nSo, there are 11 outcomes in event $B$.\n$$ P(B) = \\frac{11}{36} $$\n\n**Step 3: Determine the number of outcomes for event $A \\cap B$ (both rolls are 6).**\nThe event $A$ is that both rolls are 6. The only outcome is $(6,6)$.\nSince $(6,6)$ is included in event $B$, $A \\cap B$ is simply $A$.\nThere is 1 outcome in event $A$.\n$$ P(A \\cap B) = \\frac{1}{36} $$\n\n**Step 4: Calculate the conditional probability.**\n$$ P(A | B) = \\frac{P(A \\cap B)}{P(B)} = \\frac{1/36}{11/36} = \\frac{1}{11} $$\n\n1/11",
